# Force LF in the working tree on every platform. The toolchain expects LF
# everywhere — rustfmt (`newline_style = "Unix"`), taplo, and `.editorconfig`.
# Without this, Git's `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) checks files out
# as CRLF, so formatting checks and Lefthook can fail on a native Windows host
# even though the repository blobs are LF.
# `text=auto` still lets Git auto-detect and leave binaries alone.
#
# Existing Windows clones: after pulling this file, run once to refresh the working
# tree to LF:  git add --renormalize . && git checkout .
* text=auto eol=lf

# These are calendars, and a calendar's line endings are its syntax: RFC 5545 terminates a
# content line with `CRLF`, and docs/adr/0001 states the round trip over the octets a
# producer wrote. Rewriting LF <-> CRLF inside one rewrites the thing under test.
**/tests/fixtures/** -text

# `tests/corpus/` is where a calendar this repository did not write goes. It is protected
# here and deliberately absent from REUSE.toml: a file under it carries its own `.license`
# naming whoever holds it, and `just reuse` fails until one does. Under `tests/fixtures/`
# the blanket annotation would absorb it into a copyright claim nobody checked.
**/tests/corpus/** -text
